    "note": "Tama at the sandpit, filled the red bucket with the scoop, tipped it out, did it again and again. Said \"more sand\" when it was empty. Carried it to the water trough and poured it in and watched it sink.",
    "story": "More sand\n\nLearning Story\nTama worked at the sandpit with the red bucket and scoop. He filled the bucket with sand, tipped it out, then filled it again. He repeated this action again and again, using the scoop to move the sand into the bucket and watching what happened when he emptied it.\n\nWhen the bucket was empty, Tama used his words to ask for what he wanted. He said, \"more sand\". This was a clear and useful message. Tama was letting us know what he needed to keep his play going.\n\nTama then carried the bucket to the water trough. He poured the sand into the water and watched it sink. This changed the play from filling and emptying to testing what sand does in water. He was using his body, his tools, and his attention to explore a simple idea in a hands-on way.\n\nWhat learning we noticed\nTama was practising repeated actions, filling, tipping, carrying, and pouring. Repeating the same action helped him notice what stayed the same and what changed. His words, \"more sand\", showed emerging communication as he named what he wanted.\n\nWe also noticed Tama beginning to build a working theory about sand and water. He did not just pour and move on. He watched the sand sink, which tells us he was paying attention to the result of his action.\n\nCurriculum links\nMana aotūroa | Exploration: Tama used active exploration as he scooped, tipped, poured, and watched the sand in the water. He was learning through using his senses and testing what materials do.\n\nMana reo | Communication: Tama used a short phrase to express what he wanted when the bucket was empty. His words helped him continue his play.\n\nWhere to next / Responding\nWe can support Tama by keeping simple scooping and pouring tools available at the sandpit and water trough. We can use clear words alongside his play, such as full, empty, pour, sink, more, and again.\n\nWe will continue to notice what Tama does after he pours the sand into water. Does he repeat it, try a different container, or look for more sand? This will help us follow his thinking as he explores cause and effect.\n\nWhānau link\nYou might notice Tama using similar ideas at home when he fills, tips, pours, or asks for more.",
